traceability.evidence:
Story file exists: docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md (confirmed via check_file_exists).,Core parser implementation is in src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-internal.ts and src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-utils.ts, used by src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts.,normalizeCommentLine implementation (src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-internal.ts):

```ts
export function normalizeCommentLine(rawLine: string): string {
  const trimmed = rawLine.trim();
  if (!trimmed) {
    return "";
  }

  const annotationMatch = trimmed.match(/@story\b|@req\b|@supports\b/);
  if (!annotationMatch || annotationMatch.index === undefined) {
    const withoutLeadingStar = trimmed.replace(/^\*\s?/, "");
    return withoutLeadingStar;
  }

  return trimmed.slice(annotationMatch.index);
}
```
- This only detects `@story`, `@req`, and `@supports` and does NOT treat other JSDoc tags (e.g. `@param`, `@returns`) as boundaries.
- For a JSDoc line like `* @param {object} data`, normalizeCommentLine returns `"@param {object} data"`, which will be treated as a continuation line by the downstream logic.,processCommentLine implementation (src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-internal.ts, via src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts):

```ts
function processCommentLine({
  normalized,
  pending,
  context,
  comment,
  options,
}: {
  normalized: string;
  pending: PendingAnnotation | null;
  context: any;
  comment: any;
  options: ResolvedAnnotationOptions;
}): PendingAnnotation | null {
  if (!normalized) {
    return pending;
  }

  const isStory = /@story\b/.test(normalized);
  const isReq = /@req\b/.test(normalized);
  const isImplements = /@supports\b/.test(normalized);

  if (isImplements) {
    const implementsValue = normalized.replace(/^@supports\b/, "").trim();
    validateImplementsAnnotation(context, comment, implementsValue, options);
    return pending;
  }

  if (isStory || isReq) {
    finalizePendingAnnotation(context, comment, options, pending);
    const value = normalized.replace(/^@story\b|^@req\b/, "").trim();
    return {
      type: isStory ? "story" : "req",
      value,
      hasValue: value.trim().length > 0,
    };
  }

  if (pending) {
    const continuation = normalized.trim();
    if (!continuation) {
      return pending;
    }
    const updatedValue = pending.value
      ? `${pending.value} ${continuation}`
      : continuation;
    return {
      ...pending,
      value: updatedValue,
      hasValue: pending.hasValue || continuation.length > 0,
    };
  }

  return pending;
}
```
- Any non-empty line that does NOT contain `@story`, `@req`, or `@supports` is treated as a continuation of the current pending annotation.
- That includes lines starting with any other JSDoc tag (e.g. `@param`, `@returns`, `@throws`), which directly conflicts with the story’s requirement that JSDoc tags act as boundaries.,Whitespace collapsing (src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-utils.ts):

```ts
export function collapseAnnotationValue(value: string): string {
  return value.replace(/\s+/g, "");
}
```
- This removes ALL whitespace (including newlines) inside an annotation value.
- Combined with the current continuation logic, a sequence like:
  ```js
  /**
   * @req REQ-OPTIMIZATION
   * @param {object} data
   */
  ```
  is parsed into a single pending `@req` value of `"REQ-OPTIMIZATION @param {object} data"`, which is then collapsed to `"REQ-OPTIMIZATION@param{object}data"` before validation — exactly the incorrect behavior described in the story’s Problem Context.,There is no implementation of a generic JSDoc tag boundary detector such as the story’s suggested `/^\s*\*?\s*@\w+/` pattern. The only tag checks in the parser are `@story`, `@req`, and `@supports`.,Tests for valid-annotation-format (tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts) include only these JSDoc-related valid cases:

```ts
{
  name: "[REQ-FORMAT-SPECIFICATION] valid block annotations (single-line values)",
  code: `/**
 * @story docs/stories/005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION.story.md
 * @req REQ-VALID-EXAMPLE
 */`,
},
{
  name: "[REQ-MULTILINE-SUPPORT] valid multi-line @story annotation value in block comment",
  code: `/**
 * @story docs/stories/005.0-
 * DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION.story.md
 */`,
},
{
  name: "[REQ-MULTILINE-SUPPORT] valid multi-line @req annotation value in block comment",
  code: `/**
 * @req REQ-
 * EXAMPLE
 */`,
},
{
  name: "[REQ-FLEXIBLE-PARSING] valid JSDoc-style comment with leading stars and spacing",
  code: `/**
 *   @story   docs/stories/005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION.story.md
 *   @req   REQ-FLEXIBLE-PARSING
 */`,
},
```
- None of these tests cover coexistence of traceability tags with other JSDoc tags like `@param` or `@returns`.
- There are no tests matching the story’s specific examples with `@param` / other JSDoc tags interleaved.,Global and targeted searches show no references to the story or its requirement IDs in code or tests:
- No `022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE` or `REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE` appears in src/ or tests/ (search_file_content on those paths is limited by ignore rules, but the Jest output lists all describe blocks and none mention Story 022.0).
- Test output from `npm test -- --runInBand --verbose` shows stories 001.0 through 021.0 referenced in describe blocks, but no suite for Story 022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.,Documentation for valid-annotation-format (docs/rules/valid-annotation-format.md) mentions JSDoc comments and multiline behavior in general but does NOT show any examples of traceability annotations coexisting with other JSDoc tags (`@param`, `@returns`, etc.) as required by this story. It also does not mention Story 022.0 or its requirements.,All existing tests pass (`npm test -- --runInBand --verbose`), but there are no tests that exercise or validate the JSDoc tag-coexistence scenarios described in this story, so the known bug can still be present without causing test failures.
